AUDIO: 'Now installing this, as I said, is super simple. You literally just use the shadcn CLI, and this will add the code to your code base. So you can go in there later and ask Claude or something to change the model for you, and the effect should just work. It also'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'installs the dependencies for you like three.js, which this component uses. And that\'s something important to know about this library. It\'s sort of split into two components. First, we have the ones that use WebGL and three.js, like this'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one, that should work on most modern browsers as they support those technologies. Then we also have the ones that use HTML and Canvas, like this bend one. You can see as I scroll here, the HTML is actually curving around the page. And this is an effect that can'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only be achieved with HTML and Canvas, which is actually an experimental browser capability, which requires a Chrome feature flag to be enabled. But before I explain what HTML in Canvas is, one really cool thing to know about'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these effects is they don\'t just break on uncompatible browsers. They actually have fallbacks. So I switch over to Firefox here, which doesn\'t support HTML and Canvas and doesn\'t have the feature flag, and you can see that the bend one here is just a normal webpage now. It'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
