[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HTML and Canvas before. So if you want a detailed look, go ahead and watch that video after this and subscribe to stay up to date with AI and software development. But to give you a quick recap, normally if you want a GPU effect over your content on the web, you have'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two options. Option one is that you rebuild your content inside of a canvas. So now you\'re drawing your own text and writing your own layout engine and you can\'t use components like select and that means accessibility, search, translation, find on page, all of that'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely breaks. This is why companies like Figma had to build a browser inside of a browser and why Google Docs runs its own layout engine. Option two is that you essentially screenshot your DOM and draw a picture and that\'s what a'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'library like HTML to Canvas does and it\'s essentially just a re-implementation of DOM and CSS rendering inside of a canvas but it doesn\'t support everything. So both of those options aren\'t so great and have a lot of tradeoffs and this is what HTML'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Canvas is trying to fix. It\'s currently a WYCGI proposal but it adds a method called draw element image and that takes a real DOM element and draws it inside of your canvas. It also has a method called text element image 2D for WebGL and copy element image to texture'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for WebGPU so you can actually pull a live DOM element in as a texture to a 3D scene. The key tool of it though is that the HTML element stays as a real element. It stays completely in the accessibility tree, it stays selectable,'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and when it updates, the canvas updates as well. The real TLDR of HTML and canvas is just think of it as shaders for HTML or a reincarnation of flash. If you\'re still a little bit confused, as I'

